Bangalore India Silicon Valley a  high-flying IT hub for multinational  corporations located on the city's outer  edges but Bangalore is also home to  severe water issues polluted lakes that  froth over with toxic foam made up of  chemicals and raw sewage an unchecked  tech driven urban growth that has left  many of the city's water grid among  those affected is Sadashiva Ramakrishna  he's a successful IT professional with  his own company but his apartment  complex gets no piped water it's a  problem faced by many in Bangalore tech  community and Sadashiva has their  patience with civic authorities is  wearing thin when we moved to the area  the expectation was definitely like no  regular water supply and regular  sewerage connection dissatisfaction has  grown and if it continues yes definitely  I could see that people are getting on  the edge many in Bangalore are now  reliant on private tankers which get  their supply from bore wells as deep as  1500 feet the practice is depleting  local water reserves  leading many worried the city could soon  run out of the resource altogether but  conservationists vishwanadh Serkan taya  believes the situation is not so dire  he's been studying bangalore's water for  over 30 years and says the crisis has  been blown out of proportion because of  its effect on the IT community these are  the people with the loudest voices and  sometimes their voices distort the  problem definition itself it's made out  as to the resources running out it's not  so equity is the issue and allocation is  the issue which will combat  infrastructure some of that  infrastructure could lie here in  traditional open wells like this one  which is around a hundred years old  there are approximately 10,000 of these  in Bangalore today and unlike the city's  new wave of bore wells they don't go as  deep into the ground wells like these  are built and maintained by traditional  well diggers like Ramakrishna  who lives in a village outside the city  Ramakrishna is a fourth generation well  digger and his communities local  know-how is centuries-old he believes it  can be used to help tackle the city's  modern-day problems earlier we used to  get all our water from open wells people  forgot about them but now they're  becoming more aware and think we should  use them nearer ok we keep using bore  wells the city will run out of water  alongside water conservationists  Ramakrishna is now involved in a  campaign to recharge the city's wells it  would replenish shallow groundwater  aquifers with rainwater the process is  more environmentally friendly than  drilling bore wells deep into the ground  and the campaign aims to have a million  functional open wells by the time it's  done Ramakrishna and the community he  belongs to represent a more sustainable  approach to water conservation and if  Bangalore draws upon their traditional  expertise it might just stem its water  crisis before the taps run dry
 

